## Start

```bash
cp .env.example .env
{{if ne .Preset.Name "minimal-api"}}docker compose up -d postgres{{if eq .Preset.Name "event-driven-api"}} redis kafka worker{{end}}{{if or (eq .Preset.Name "production-api") (eq .Preset.Name "event-driven-api")}} prometheus otel-collector{{end}}
export DATABASE_URL='postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/{{.Config.Project.Name}}?sslmode=disable'
make tools
make migrate-up
make generate
{{end}}{{if or (eq .Preset.Name "production-api") (eq .Preset.Name "event-driven-api")}}export JWT_SECRET="$(openssl rand -base64 48)"
{{end}}make verify
make run
```

{{if or (eq .Preset.Name "production-api") (eq .Preset.Name "event-driven-api")}}## Create the first user

Create a bcrypt hash locally:

```bash
PASSWORD_HASH="$(go run ./cmd/hash-password 'change-this-password')"
```

Insert an initial administrator into the development database:

```bash
docker compose exec -T postgres psql \
  -U postgres \
  -d {{.Config.Project.Name}} \
  -v password_hash="$PASSWORD_HASH" <<'SQL'
INSERT INTO users (id, email, password_hash, roles)
VALUES (
  '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001',
  'admin@example.com',
  :'password_hash',
  ARRAY['admin']
)
ON CONFLICT (email) DO NOTHING;
SQL
```

Do not reuse the development password, UUID, or JWT secret in production.
Use a secret manager and set `AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=true` behind HTTPS.

## Authentication

```bash
curl -i -c cookies.txt \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"email":"admin@example.com","password":"change-this-password"}' \
  http://localhost:{{.Config.API.Port}}/auth/login
```

The response contains the short-lived access token. The refresh token is stored
only in an HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookie.

Refresh and logout:

```bash
curl -i -b cookies.txt -c cookies.txt -X POST \
  http://localhost:{{.Config.API.Port}}/auth/refresh

curl -i -b cookies.txt -c cookies.txt -X POST \
  http://localhost:{{.Config.API.Port}}/auth/logout
```

Protected routes require:

```text
Authorization: Bearer <access-token>
```

The generated example includes an admin-only endpoint:

```text
GET /admin/ping
```

## Observability

```text
GET /metrics
OTLP gRPC collector: localhost:4317
Prometheus UI:       http://localhost:9090
```

`pprof` is disabled by default. When explicitly enabled, it listens on the
private administration address configured by `PPROF_ADDRESS` (default
`127.0.0.1:6060`) rather than the public API router.

The development OpenTelemetry Collector uses a debug exporter. Connect it to a
trace backend such as Tempo or Jaeger before relying on trace retention.

{{end}}{{if eq .Preset.Name "event-driven-api"}}## Event-driven runtime

The API and worker are separate processes:

```bash
make run
make run-worker
```

The Outbox worker claims rows with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, publishes them to
Kafka, and marks them as published. After the configured attempt limit, the
event is routed to `<topic>{{.Config.Messaging.DLQSuffix}}`.

Redis provides an idempotency store for consumers. The generated Kafka consumer
commits offsets only after successful handling or successful DLQ publication.

Development services:

```text
PostgreSQL: localhost:5432
Redis:      localhost:6379
Kafka:      localhost:9092
```

Kubernetes manifests assume PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka, and OpenTelemetry are
provided as managed or separately deployed services. Replace image names and create the Kubernetes Secret before applying:

```bash
cp deployments/k8s/11-secret.example deployments/k8s/11-secret.yaml
# Edit deployments/k8s/11-secret.yaml and replace all placeholders.
make k8s-validate
make k8s-apply
```

Build both runtime and migration images:

```bash
make docker-build VERSION=dev
```

